.TH BIN 3
.SH NAME
bin, lib \- programs and files the kernel image carries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind #b /bin
.nf

.BI /bin/ program1
.BI /bin/ program2
\&...
.fi
.PP
.B bind #l /lib
.nf

.B /lib/tests/tools.rc
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Bin
and
.I lib
each serve a flat, read-only tree of files baked into the kernel image at
build time: a program in
.BR bin ,
a file that is not a program in
.BR lib .
Both are what a machine has before any disk is mounted, which is why the
kernel's first program comes from them:
.B /bin/rc /lib/init
(see
.IR intro (1)).
.PP
A file under
.B /bin
is a program in the
.B VECTRA01
or
.B VECTRA02
image format (see
.IR vx (6)).
.IR Exec (2)
reads a file by that format regardless of where it came from, so a program
under
.B /bin
differs from one loaded off a disk only in which file server answered the
read.
.PP
.B /lib/tests/tools.rc
is the script
.I verify
runs the shell against at boot (see
.IR verify (8)).
.PP
Both trees are read-only: creating, removing or renaming a file in either
fails with
.BR EPERM .
The programs and files a machine actually runs come from the boot disk's
.B /n/esp/vectra/bin
and
.B /n/esp/vectra/lib
instead, bound in front of these two once
.IR fatfs (4)
is mounted (see
.IR intro (1)),
so a build can change what
.B /bin
and
.B /lib
mean without a kernel rebuild.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/user/image.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/lib.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (3),
.IR intro (1),
.IR root (3),
.IR exec (2),
.IR fork (2),
.IR vx (6),
.IR fatfs (4),
.IR verify (8)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Creating, removing or renaming a file under
.B /bin
or
.B /lib
fails with
.BR EPERM .
